@@s001 Derivatives: the rules
@@s002 dato:x,ok:values forward,ora:local derivatives and product
@@s003 y = (3x + 1)⁴ is a chain: first the inner function u = 3x + 1, then the outer one y = u⁴.
@@s004 Forward with x = 1: u = 3·1 + 1 = <b>4</b>…
@@s005 …and y = 4⁴ = <b>256</b>.
@@s006 Local derivative of the outer node: dy/du = 4u³ = 4 · 64 = <b>256</b>.
@@s007 Local derivative of the inner node: du/dx = <b>3</b>.
@@s008 Chain rule: the derivatives are <b>multiplied</b> along the path. dy/dx = 256 · 3 = <b>768</b> = 12(3x + 1)³ at x = 1. It is backpropagation in miniature.
@@s009 dato:f(x) = x²,ora:secant,ok:tangent at x = 1
@@s010 ora:the exponent coming down,ok:the result
@@s011 dato:u and u′,due:v and v′,ok:the sum of the two cross terms
@@s012 dato:road through x,due:road through y,ok:sum of the roads
@@s013 dato:σ(z),ok:σ′(z) = σ(1 − σ),ora:one point
@@s014 Derivatives: the rules
@@s015 The rules to compute derivatives by hand, with worked examples. They serve for the Grasple exercises (<i>Defining the derivative</i>, <i>Differentiation rules</i>, <i>Product rule</i>, <i>Chain rule</i>), for the gradients of week 8 and for the training of the neural networks of week 14. Every result is checked with sympy.
@@s016 What the derivative is
@@s017 The <b>derivative</b> of a function f at a point x, written <span class="m">f′(x)</span> (read "f prime of x") or <span class="m">df/dx</span>, is the <b>slope</b> of the curve at that point: how much f changes for a very small change of x. Positive where the curve rises, negative where it falls, zero at the top or at the bottom.
@@s018 It is built with the <b>difference quotient</b>: two nearby points are taken, x and x + h, and the slope of the line that joins them (the <b>secant</b>) is computed, that is (f(x + h) − f(x)) / h. Making the second point approach the first (h ever smaller) the secant becomes the <b>tangent</b>, and its slope is the derivative:
@@s019 It is the Grasple exercise <i>Defining the derivative</i>. In practice the limit is not computed every time: one uses the rules of the sections below. In the images (week 8) one uses instead precisely the difference quotient with h = 1 pixel: the <b>discrete derivative</b> f(x + 1) − f(x).
@@s020 From the secant to the tangent, f(x) = x² at x = 1
@@s021 The derivatives to know by heart
@@s022 Example
@@s023 c (constant)
@@s024 ln x
@@s025 sin x
@@s026 cos x
@@s027 cos x
@@s028 −sin x
@@s029 Sum and constants
@@s030 The derivative of a sum is the sum of the derivatives; a constant that multiplies stays outside. (3x² − 2x + 1)′ = 3·2x − 2·1 + 0 = <b>6x − 2</b>.
@@s031 The power rule, and the sum
@@s032 Product
@@s033 (u · v)′ = u′ v + u v′   with f(x) = x² sin x
@@s034 One recognises the two factors.
@@s035 One differentiates each on its own.
@@s036 "I differentiate the first and leave the second, plus I leave the first and differentiate the second". It is <b>not</b> u′ · v′.
@@s037 Check on a point.
@@s038 Quotient
@@s039 <span class="m">(u / v)′ = (u′v − u v′) / v²</span>. Example: (x / (x + 1))′ = (1·(x + 1) − x·1) / (x + 1)² = <b>1 / (x + 1)²</b>.
@@s040 (x² · sin x)′
@@s041 The chain rule
@@s042 It is needed when a function sits <b>inside</b> another: f(x) = g(h(x)). It is the most important rule of the course: the backpropagation of neural networks is the chain rule applied many times.
@@s043 (3x + 1)⁴
@@s044 One names the inner function.
@@s045 Derivative of the outside, with u in place of x.
@@s046 Derivative of the inside.
@@s047 They are multiplied. In Leibniz's notation the du "cancel": a good reminder, not a proof.
@@s048 The chain as a graph, with x = 1
@@s049 inside u
@@s050 ln(1 + x²)
@@s051 (y − wx)² with respect to <b>w</b>
@@s052 y − wx
@@s053 2(y − wx) · (−x) = −2x(y − wx)
@@s054 The last line is the derivative of the least squares loss for one point, with respect to the weight: it is the one used by gradient descent.
@@s055 Chain with several variables
@@s056 Example of DW 25: z = x + y²,  with x = t² and y = sin t
@@s057 t influences z by <b>two roads</b>.
@@s058 One term for each road, then they are summed.
@@s059 ∂z/∂x = 1, ∂z/∂y = 2y, dx/dt = 2t, dy/dt = cos t.
@@s060 One substitutes y = sin t back. With three roads (the other example of the book, z = u + v·w) the terms are three: 3t² + eᵗ ln(t + 1) + eᵗ/(t + 1).
@@s061 Two roads from t to z
@@s062 Two derivatives that come back often
@@s063 The sigmoid: σ(z) = 1 / (1 + e⁻ᶻ)
@@s064 It is obtained with the chain (verified with sympy). Handy: the derivative is computed from the value of the sigmoid itself.
@@s065 It is the steepest point. Far from 0 the derivative tends to 0: the sigmoid "saturates". It serves for logistic regression (week 10) and networks (week 14).
@@s066 ReLU
@@s067 ReLU(z) = max(0, z): derivative <b>0</b> for z &lt; 0 and <b>1</b> for z &gt; 0 (at 0 one of the two is chosen by convention).
@@s068 Partial derivatives and the gradient
@@s069 With several variables one differentiates with respect to one at a time, treating the others as numbers; the gradient is the vector of all these derivatives. Explained with examples on the page <a href="w08-derivate-bordi.html">Derivatives, gradients and edges</a>.
@@s070 The sigmoid and its derivative
